1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
To make peace, scientists built the ultimate weapon of war.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
To fight fascism, they unleashed the power of the atom.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Theoretical brilliance and engineering grit gave birth to an ethical nightmare, the atom bomb.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Now, the Manhattan Project on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,640
At 5.30 a.m., July 16, 1945, scientists and dignitaries

6
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:38,640
awaited the detonation of the first atomic bomb

7
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:41,540
in a desolate area of the New Mexico desert,

8
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:48,560
known aptly as Jornada del Muerto, or Journey of Death.

9
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:51,260
They had taken bets on how much power their creation might

10
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:54,460
unleash, but many wondered whether the weapon would work

11
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:56,160
at all.

12
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:58,660
There was, however, one certainty.

13
00:00:58,661 --> 00:01:03,660
If the atomic bomb detonated, the world would never be the same.

14
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:07,500
This story is the great tragic epic of the 20th century.

15
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:09,440
If I were going to give it a theme,

16
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:12,500
the theme would be humankind invents

17
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,240
the means of its own destruction.

18
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:18,980
And in order to do so, scientists, engineers,

19
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:21,420
and the army teamed up in an effort dubbed

20
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:23,420
the Manhattan Project.

21
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:25,220
In the short span of two years, they

22
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:28,660
built an industrial complex with sites across the country

23
00:01:28,661 --> 00:01:34,160
country that would rival the size of the automotive industry.

24
00:01:34,161 --> 00:01:37,860
To manufacture fuel for the bomb, they built the fifth largest town

25
00:01:37,861 --> 00:01:41,260
in the state of Tennessee that consumed one-tenth of the

26
00:01:41,261 --> 00:01:44,800
electrical power generated in the United States.

27
00:01:44,801 --> 00:01:48,620
The bomb builders spent well over $2 billion, almost $30

28
00:01:48,621 --> 00:01:52,400
billion in today's market, to detonate the first man-made

29
00:01:52,401 --> 00:01:56,660
atomic explosion just 28 months after scientists set foot in a

30
00:01:56,661 --> 00:02:02,160
at the Manhattan Project's Los Alamos, New Mexico facility.

31
00:02:02,161 --> 00:02:07,100
Nonetheless, that July morning in 1945, many of the world's top

32
00:02:07,101 --> 00:02:10,900
physicists watched anxiously at the test site 200 miles south

33
00:02:10,901 --> 00:02:14,620
of Los Alamos, awaiting the results of their labors.

34
00:02:14,621 --> 00:02:18,220
Five, four, three, two, one.

35
00:02:18,221 --> 00:02:22,020
But this countdown had really begun 12 years before, when

36
00:02:22,021 --> 00:02:24,960
many of these scientists escaped the coming of fascism

37
00:02:24,961 --> 00:02:29,760
to Europe. Once Hitler took power in 1933,

38
00:02:29,860 --> 00:02:32,760
the Jews of Europe, especially the Jews of Germany,

39
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:38,740
were under a direct threat. And some of the best scientists

40
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:42,180
in the country happened to be Jewish.

41
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:45,520
Hans Bethe was one. Edward Teller was one.

42
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:49,760
Leo Szilard was one. Einstein was one.

43
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:54,420
And one by one, they fled Europe to the United States.

44
00:02:54,421 --> 00:02:57,920
Scientists found refuge at American universities.

45
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:02,420
In 1938, dramatic news arrived from Germany.

46
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:04,760
German scientists had split the atom

47
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:07,560
by bombarding uranium with neutrons, which caused

48
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:10,660
instability in the uranium nucleus.

49
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:14,080
During this split, mass was lost and was converted

50
00:03:14,180 --> 00:03:17,980
into kinetic energy. They had discovered fission

51
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:21,780
and with it opened a Pandora's box.

52
00:03:21,781 --> 00:03:26,260
If each of those neutrons goes into another uranium nucleus

53
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:29,260
and causes fission, then the first fission

54
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:32,820
has led to more than two fissions in the next generation.

55
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:35,700
And you can see that each successive generation

56
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:40,240
has many more neutrons, and this causes an explosive chain

57
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:41,360
reaction.

58
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:43,240
If two masses of highly fissionable

59
00:03:43,340 --> 00:03:45,940
uranium in the form of a sphere and a plug

60
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:50,780
could be brought together with sufficient speed inside a bomb,

61
00:03:50,781 --> 00:03:55,180
an exponentially increasing chain reaction with explosive force

62
00:03:55,181 --> 00:03:59,780
would result. The implications of the discovery

63
00:03:59,781 --> 00:04:03,380
of the fission of uranium would be obvious to scientists in Nazi

64
00:04:03,381 --> 00:04:07,940
Germany, and this frightened Leo Szilard, a 40-year-old émigré

65
00:04:07,941 --> 00:04:12,020
physicist from Hungary. Politically astute, Szilard

66
00:04:12,021 --> 00:04:15,100
wanted to warn President Roosevelt, but knew that he lacked

67
00:04:15,101 --> 00:04:20,660
the stature to do so. Imagine men with heavy foreign

68
00:04:20,661 --> 00:04:24,860
say they have figured out a way to make a bomb no bigger

69
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:29,000
than an ordinary bomb that could blow up a city.

70
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:31,300
You might well think they were crackpots

71
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:33,400
and throw them out of your office.

72
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:35,780
Zillard wrote a letter to the president

73
00:04:35,880 --> 00:04:40,920
and sought a prominent ally to sign it, Albert Einstein.

74
00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:44,380
Zillard, who didn't drive a car, enlisted the help of his friend

75
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:48,580
Edward Teller, an eminent theoretical physicist,

76
00:04:48,581 --> 00:04:53,020
to drive him to Einstein's summer house on Long Island.

77
00:05:05,020 --> 00:05:09,140
On October 11, 1939, in response to Einstein

78
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:12,140
and Szilard's letter of warning, President Roosevelt

79
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:15,640
formed the Advisory Committee on Uranium.

80
00:05:15,641 --> 00:05:18,980
But this abstract new discovery became a low priority

81
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:22,220
for a leadership distracted by a world war that was now

82
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:23,320
two months old.

83
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:30,180
A recent refugee of that war, 37-year-old physicist

84
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:35,120
Enrico Fermi narrowly escaped with his Jewish wife, Laura.

85
00:05:35,220 --> 00:05:37,420
They used the opportunity of going to Sweden

86
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:41,360
to collect the Nobel Prize to take, to go from there

87
00:05:41,460 --> 00:05:43,340
and then go on to the United States,

88
00:05:43,341 --> 00:05:47,040
so that the fascist authorities in Italy at that time

89
00:05:47,140 --> 00:05:49,180
didn't know that they were actually not going

90
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:51,080
to be returning to Italy.

91
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:53,620
Together with Leo Szilard at Columbia,

92
00:05:53,720 --> 00:05:56,620
Fermi tackled the first obstacle to the bomb,

93
00:05:56,720 --> 00:05:59,320
the question of whether or not a sustained chain reaction

94
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:03,960
could be induced in a uranium reactor.

95
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:06,600
Without the successively doubling power of the chain

96
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:10,360
reaction, a bomb would be an impossibility.

97
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:12,100
Fermi was one of the few scientists

98
00:06:12,101 --> 00:06:15,600
who was talented both in theory and in practice.

99
00:06:15,700 --> 00:06:18,540
He loved getting his hands dirty.

100
00:06:18,640 --> 00:06:23,840
Leo Szilard was the antithesis of Enrico Fermi.

101
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:26,840
Szilard usually slept late.

102
00:06:26,940 --> 00:06:30,260
He soaked in the bathtub to get fresh ideas.

103
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:32,260
While in the bathtub, Szilard remained

104
00:06:32,360 --> 00:06:36,160
focused on the competition, the German bomb effort.

105
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:39,200
He convinced scientists in the US whose community thrived

106
00:06:39,201 --> 00:06:43,440
on openness, to censor their own papers so that they would not

107
00:06:43,441 --> 00:06:47,440
inadvertently help the Germans. As the US bomb effort was

108
00:06:47,441 --> 00:06:52,440
taking its first tentative steps, news that the Kaiser Wilhelm

109
00:06:52,441 --> 00:06:55,640
Institute in Berlin had begun actively pursuing uranium

110
00:06:55,641 --> 00:07:01,360
research rippled through the American scientific community.

111
00:07:01,361 --> 00:07:04,360
It would take nothing short of a disaster to move the president

112
00:07:04,361 --> 00:07:10,760
take decisive action, and a disaster was not far off.

113
00:07:10,761 --> 00:07:14,900
Prior to 1933, Germany produced the most Nobel laureate

114
00:07:14,901 --> 00:07:18,800
scientists. After that date, scientists in the United States

115
00:07:18,801 --> 00:07:25,520
received the most honors. On December 7, 1941, the

116
00:07:25,521 --> 00:07:29,760
Japanese attacked Pearl Harbor, sinking 19 US naval vessels and

117
00:07:29,761 --> 00:07:32,360
killing more than 2,000 soldiers.

118
00:07:32,361 --> 00:07:34,760
No matter how long it may take us.

119
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:38,060
The next day, the US declared war on Japan.

120
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:40,860
World War II was no longer just Europe's war.

121
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:47,100
And their righteous might will win through to absolute victory.

122
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:50,220
America's entry into the war had a galvanizing effect

123
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:53,620
on its moribund atomic bomb effort.

124
00:07:53,720 --> 00:07:56,660
President Roosevelt approved production of the weapon

125
00:07:56,760 --> 00:08:00,360
and turned the nuclear program over to the army.

126
00:08:00,361 --> 00:08:03,560
Brigadier General Leslie R. Groves, an expert engineer

127
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:06,060
and administrator, was placed at the head of the Manhattan

128
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:10,900
Engineer District, named after the project's initial headquarters

129
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,600
in New York City.

130
00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:16,600
GENERAL GROVES, HAVING JUST FINISHED BUILDING THE PENTAGON,

131
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:20,020
WAS ABSOLUTELY DISGUSTED TO BE ASSIGNED THE MANHATTAN PROJECT,

132
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:21,720
AS IT CAME TO BE CALLED.

133
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:23,220
HE WAS SURE IT WAS A BOONDOGGLE.

134
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:26,820
HE WAS SURE YOU COULD NEVER DO SUCH A THING.

135
00:08:26,821 --> 00:08:30,060
But at the University of Chicago, Zillard and Fermi

136
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:34,060
were taking the first steps in proving Groves wrong.

137
00:08:34,160 --> 00:08:36,960
They were trying to demonstrate that the fission process could

138
00:08:37,060 --> 00:08:40,140
be harnessed by launching a sustained chain reaction in

139
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:42,500
uranium.

140
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:45,300
They built the first primitive reactor in a squash court

141
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:48,480
beneath the stands of the university's football stadium.

142
00:08:48,580 --> 00:08:51,620
The reactor consisted of a pile in which a fission reaction

143
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:54,480
could be initiated and controlled.

144
00:08:54,481 --> 00:08:57,880
They did it by piling layers of graphite

145
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:01,320
and then embedding balls of uranium

146
00:09:01,420 --> 00:09:04,260
so that when the neutrons started flying through,

147
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:06,400
they would be slowed by the graphite,

148
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:09,700
collide with the uranium, release more neutrons,

149
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:13,000
and those neutrons would continue in the chain reaction

150
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:14,100
pattern.

151
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:16,540
This momentous achievement, only detectable

152
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:19,840
by a Geiger counter, meant that the fission process

153
00:09:19,940 --> 00:09:21,380
could be sustained.

154
00:09:21,480 --> 00:09:24,480
Nuclear energy could be released in a controlled

155
00:09:24,481 --> 00:09:31,280
controlled way, as in a reactor, or perhaps cataclysmically, as in a bomb.

156
00:09:31,281 --> 00:09:37,720
It was so crazy that you could put a bunch of uranium together with

157
00:09:37,721 --> 00:09:42,160
graphite, and the thing would spring to life.

158
00:09:42,161 --> 00:09:45,440
The bomb effort went into high gear.

159
00:09:45,441 --> 00:09:48,680
Its first priority was the lack of weapons-grade fuel.

160
00:09:48,681 --> 00:09:52,400
Fission is more readily produced in a rare uranium isotope,

161
00:09:52,401 --> 00:09:58,300
uranium-235, that occurs naturally at a ratio of 1 to

162
00:09:58,301 --> 00:10:03,740
139 to its less useful twin, uranium-238.

163
00:10:03,741 --> 00:10:07,240
Chemically identical, the isotopes were almost impossible

164
00:10:07,241 --> 00:10:14,160
to separate. The only workable method in 1942

165
00:10:14,161 --> 00:10:18,360
was electromagnetic separation. In this process, a mass

166
00:10:18,361 --> 00:10:21,600
spectrometer used for separating electrically charged particles

167
00:10:21,601 --> 00:10:24,940
according to their mass, sent a stream of uranium atoms

168
00:10:24,941 --> 00:10:30,100
past a magnet. Atoms of the lighter isotope U-235

169
00:10:30,101 --> 00:10:33,940
would be deflected more than those of the heavier U-238,

170
00:10:33,941 --> 00:10:38,780
and would be captured one atom at a time.

171
00:10:38,781 --> 00:10:41,960
Scientists at Columbia University championed a competing mode

172
00:10:41,961 --> 00:10:45,520
of separation, gaseous diffusion.

173
00:10:45,521 --> 00:10:49,000
This method passed the isotope through a porous barrier that

174
00:10:49,001 --> 00:10:51,840
the lighter isotope from its heavier counterpart,

175
00:10:51,940 --> 00:10:54,500
but it proved to be technically challenging.

176
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:59,640
In a square centimeter, about the size of my thumbnail,

177
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:04,520
you've got to have hundreds of millions of pores,

178
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:06,980
and they have to all be the same size.

179
00:11:07,080 --> 00:11:09,380
If they're too big, the gas flows through

180
00:11:09,480 --> 00:11:11,020
without any separation.

181
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:14,100
If they're too small, the gas gets in there

182
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:18,500
and condenses on the surface, won't go through.

183
00:11:18,501 --> 00:11:22,740
So you talk about obstacles.

184
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:25,740
But if a suitable barrier could be manufactured,

185
00:11:25,840 --> 00:11:28,740
it promised a greater yield than the electromagnetic

186
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:29,740
process.

187
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:32,740
Both techniques would have to be done on a massive scale

188
00:11:32,840 --> 00:11:37,160
and would prove extremely costly.

189
00:11:37,260 --> 00:11:40,860
And if they worked, they would provide fuel for a bomb that

190
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:45,160
would be designed in a remote area of New Mexico.

191
00:11:45,161 --> 00:11:49,160
Grove selected a bomb design site in Los Alamos,

192
00:11:49,260 --> 00:11:52,960
an isolated location in the mountains at an elevation of 7,000

193
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:56,280
feet and accessible by only one road.

194
00:11:56,380 --> 00:12:00,680
General Grove wanted it to be inland too far for enemy planes

195
00:12:00,780 --> 00:12:01,780
or submarines.

196
00:12:01,880 --> 00:12:04,480
He wanted it to be beautiful where he said he could keep a

197
00:12:04,580 --> 00:12:07,180
bunch of prima donnas amused.

198
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:11,780
In November of 1942, the Army purchased 54,000 acres

199
00:12:11,781 --> 00:12:16,220
acres for $440,000 under the guise of using them as a

200
00:12:16,221 --> 00:12:20,620
demolition range. To head up the installation,

201
00:12:20,621 --> 00:12:25,620
Grove's an expert judge of men, chose a most unlikely candidate,

202
00:12:25,621 --> 00:12:30,140
J. Robert Oppenheimer. A gifted physics professor,

203
00:12:30,141 --> 00:12:33,940
Oppenheimer had a reputation for being temperamental, perhaps

204
00:12:33,941 --> 00:12:37,540
not suited to a highly stressful assignment.

205
00:12:37,541 --> 00:12:39,540
Grove was advised that Oppenheimer would be a

206
00:12:39,541 --> 00:12:43,140
People told him that Oppenheimer couldn't run a hot dog stand.

207
00:12:43,240 --> 00:12:47,440
Oppenheimer was a fascinating and complicated man.

208
00:12:47,540 --> 00:12:50,180
Fundamentally, he seems to have had some of the qualities

209
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:51,580
of an actor.

210
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:56,300
He was different things to different people.

211
00:12:56,400 --> 00:13:00,120
Oppenheimer drew luminaries like Enrico Fermi, Hans

212
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:03,060
Bethe, and Edward Teller to the facility,

213
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:07,160
as well as technicians fresh out of college.

214
00:13:07,161 --> 00:13:14,160
Laboratory personnel grew from 250 in 1943 to 2,500 in 1945.

215
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:20,600
There were lots of babies born.

216
00:13:20,601 --> 00:13:23,200
General Groves at one point asked Oppenheimer

217
00:13:23,201 --> 00:13:24,720
if he couldn't do something about that.

218
00:13:24,721 --> 00:13:27,120
He said he didn't think so.

219
00:13:27,121 --> 00:13:29,520
The one place that they had to keep expanding

220
00:13:29,521 --> 00:13:32,720
was the maternity ward at the hospital.

221
00:13:32,721 --> 00:13:35,320
During the fall of 1942,

222
00:13:35,321 --> 00:13:37,560
The theoretical physicists at Los Alamos

223
00:13:37,660 --> 00:13:40,220
began the difficult process of trying to determine

224
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:45,500
how much U-235 it would take to make a bomb.

225
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:50,040
And the scientists tell him, well, it could be X amount,

226
00:13:50,140 --> 00:13:54,600
but that's a plus or minus by a factor of 10.

227
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:56,600
Groves is absolutely staggered.

228
00:13:56,700 --> 00:14:00,820
He uses the illustration that, well, you're giving a wedding,

229
00:14:00,821 --> 00:14:05,620
and you say it's for 100 people.

230
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:08,560
But maybe 1,000 will turn up, or maybe 10.

231
00:14:08,660 --> 00:14:13,560
So how can you make any sort of plans with that range?

232
00:14:13,660 --> 00:14:17,000
This kind of uncertainty only served to fuel fears

233
00:14:17,100 --> 00:14:20,240
that the Nazis might be closer to building a bomb.

234
00:14:23,680 --> 00:14:28,140
In early fall of 1942, General Groves purchased 59,000 acres

235
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:29,520
in Oak Ridge, Tennessee.

236
00:14:29,521 --> 00:14:32,560
this would become the main site for uranium production in the

237
00:14:32,660 --> 00:14:33,520
United States.

238
00:14:36,520 --> 00:14:40,160
On December 28, 1942, President Roosevelt

239
00:14:40,260 --> 00:14:43,360
approved an additional $500 million investment in the

240
00:14:43,460 --> 00:14:47,080
Manhattan Project. The first priority, to build

241
00:14:47,180 --> 00:14:49,480
the massive industrial facilities that would produce

242
00:14:49,580 --> 00:14:52,080
the fissionable material to fuel the atom bomb.

243
00:14:55,180 --> 00:14:58,620
Robert Oppenheimer's team of physicists doubled the amount of

244
00:14:58,621 --> 00:15:02,620
of uranium-235 thought necessary to achieve critical mass and

245
00:15:02,621 --> 00:15:08,860
sustain an explosive chain reaction to 200 kilograms.

246
00:15:08,861 --> 00:15:12,300
Their calculation made without adequate samples of U-235 for

247
00:15:12,301 --> 00:15:17,260
tests would prove to be 10 times the required amount.

248
00:15:17,261 --> 00:15:21,740
The scientists at Los Alamos also studied tampers, barrier

249
00:15:21,741 --> 00:15:25,020
materials that would slow the expansion of the critical mass

250
00:15:25,021 --> 00:15:28,460
and reflect neutrons back to feed the fission process inside the

251
00:15:28,461 --> 00:15:31,460
the bomb.

252
00:15:31,560 --> 00:15:35,100
Determined to have the atom bomb ready for use in the war,

253
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:37,500
General Groves notified contractors in Oak Ridge,

254
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:41,080
Tennessee that they had a scant six months to build the massive

255
00:15:41,180 --> 00:15:44,680
electromagnetic calutrons designed by Ernest Lawrence

256
00:15:44,780 --> 00:15:48,840
at the University of California in Berkeley.

257
00:15:48,940 --> 00:15:54,160
These calutrons would be used to separate U-235 from U-238.

258
00:15:54,260 --> 00:15:56,680
Lawrence was, to some extent, an engineer.

259
00:15:56,681 --> 00:15:59,180
He was comfortable with the soldering iron and with wrenches

260
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:03,020
and with the tools of the trade, with building machines.

261
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:06,160
In February 1943, construction

262
00:16:06,260 --> 00:16:09,360
began on the calutron site that was given the deliberately

263
00:16:09,460 --> 00:16:11,900
nondescript code name, Y-12.

264
00:16:15,060 --> 00:16:16,760
These gigantic structures contained

265
00:16:16,860 --> 00:16:19,900
multiple calutrons, box-shaped collection units that were

266
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,100
configured in a racetrack layout.

267
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:24,600
A magnetic field passed throughout the entire oval

268
00:16:24,601 --> 00:16:28,400
of calutrons, causing the divergence of streams of U-235

269
00:16:28,401 --> 00:16:33,240
and U-238 so that the separated isotopes could be collected.

270
00:16:33,241 --> 00:16:38,080
A single calutron could capture a mere 10 grams of U-235 daily.

271
00:16:38,081 --> 00:16:43,380
A staggering total of 1,152 would be built by war's end.

272
00:16:46,720 --> 00:16:49,060
Because of the intense time constraints,

273
00:16:49,061 --> 00:16:52,000
Groves could not afford to build pilot plants,

274
00:16:52,001 --> 00:16:56,480
facilities to test the scaling up of the laboratory processes.

275
00:16:56,580 --> 00:16:59,900
They're physically building Oak Ridge and Y-12,

276
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,640
the electromagnetic separation plant,

277
00:17:02,740 --> 00:17:06,140
before the design drawings had even been approved.

278
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:07,580
And it's incredible that you would never

279
00:17:07,680 --> 00:17:09,280
do this in civilian life.

280
00:17:09,380 --> 00:17:11,820
And you would never do it short of a wartime emergency

281
00:17:11,920 --> 00:17:13,760
that is dire.

282
00:17:13,860 --> 00:17:18,160
They turned the Alpha 1 racetrack on to make the first uranium,

283
00:17:18,260 --> 00:17:19,620
and everything goes haywire.

284
00:17:19,621 --> 00:17:24,320
that the magnets are so strong that they pull nails out of the

285
00:17:24,420 --> 00:17:25,360
wall.

286
00:17:25,460 --> 00:17:28,260
Groves arrived and shut the plant down.

287
00:17:28,360 --> 00:17:31,360
His response was characteristic of his approach.

288
00:17:31,460 --> 00:17:34,160
Since the racetracks were not operating to capacity,

289
00:17:34,260 --> 00:17:37,780
more would have to be built to supply the bombs.

290
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:40,280
As a result, the town of Oak Ridge

291
00:17:40,380 --> 00:17:44,180
continued to grow to house an expanding workforce.

292
00:17:44,280 --> 00:17:49,620
75,000 people would ultimately come to live at the site.

293
00:17:49,621 --> 00:17:52,620
Groves used creative means to recruit them.

294
00:17:52,621 --> 00:17:55,620
It was hard to get construction workers during the war.

295
00:17:55,621 --> 00:17:58,620
His solution was to put out ads saying,

296
00:17:58,621 --> 00:18:00,620
we can't tell you what you'll be doing,

297
00:18:00,621 --> 00:18:04,620
but there'll be steak every night on the table.

298
00:18:04,621 --> 00:18:06,620
Once hired, workers were instructed to be evasive

299
00:18:06,621 --> 00:18:09,620
about what they were doing.

300
00:18:09,621 --> 00:18:11,620
If anybody asks you what you make in Oak Ridge,

301
00:18:11,621 --> 00:18:16,620
you tell them you're making the lights for the lightning bugs

302
00:18:16,621 --> 00:18:20,620
are that you're making the holes for the doughnuts.

303
00:18:20,621 --> 00:18:23,120
Ha ha.

304
00:18:23,121 --> 00:18:25,620
Meanwhile at Los Alamos, design work

305
00:18:25,621 --> 00:18:30,460
was underway on the gadget as the test bomb came to be known.

306
00:18:30,461 --> 00:18:33,080
The main obstacle was how to quickly assemble

307
00:18:33,081 --> 00:18:39,080
two smaller subcritical masses into one larger explosive one.

308
00:18:39,081 --> 00:18:43,080
The bomb design that they came up with was a gun design.

309
00:18:43,081 --> 00:18:46,580
Inside the bomb, a cannon would fire one piece of rifle

310
00:18:46,581 --> 00:18:50,580
fuel into another at 3,000 feet per second.

311
00:18:50,581 --> 00:18:53,220
The pieces would have to come together fast enough to prevent

312
00:18:53,221 --> 00:18:57,220
spontaneously emitted neutrons from melting the fuel, causing

313
00:18:57,221 --> 00:19:00,820
the bomb to fizzle rather than explode.

314
00:19:00,821 --> 00:19:03,060
But the engineering aspects were daunting.

315
00:19:03,061 --> 00:19:05,060
It's one thing to say you can shoot a piece of uranium at a

316
00:19:05,061 --> 00:19:06,900
second piece, but how do you do it?

317
00:19:06,901 --> 00:19:09,300
How fast does it have to go? How do you stop it at the end?

318
00:19:09,301 --> 00:19:12,740
How do you keep it together long enough as a mass so that it does

319
00:19:12,741 --> 00:19:15,540
go high order and give you an atomic bomb?

320
00:19:15,541 --> 00:19:18,540
Oppenheimer led the effort to overcome these obstacles

321
00:19:18,640 --> 00:19:22,840
by setting an example for his team at Los Alamos.

322
00:19:22,940 --> 00:19:24,880
In the middle of the night, something could be done.

323
00:19:24,980 --> 00:19:26,680
In Wood Walker, just at the right time,

324
00:19:26,780 --> 00:19:28,080
to see how it went.

325
00:19:28,180 --> 00:19:30,580
He was interested in everything, in the explosives,

326
00:19:30,680 --> 00:19:34,000
in the mechanics, in the chemistry,

327
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:36,500
in the bomb itself, of course.

328
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:38,900
And this is what makes all the big laboratory of thousands

329
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,900
of people working together cohere.

330
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,500
Oppenheimer was living on coffee and martinis

331
00:19:44,501 --> 00:19:47,260
and martinis and cigarettes.

332
00:19:47,261 --> 00:19:50,100
Rose was eating Whitlam and chocolate samplers

333
00:19:50,101 --> 00:19:53,080
and sleeping soundly, whereas Oppenheimer was anguished.

334
00:19:53,081 --> 00:19:55,120
You know, they were just so different.

335
00:19:55,121 --> 00:19:57,780
But they seemed to be able to mesh,

336
00:19:57,781 --> 00:20:00,680
and they certainly had a common goal.

337
00:20:00,681 --> 00:20:04,020
And this common goal was about to reach a new level.

338
00:20:04,021 --> 00:20:07,320
As scientists refined the design of the gadget,

339
00:20:07,321 --> 00:20:09,460
a new man-made element, plutonium,

340
00:20:09,461 --> 00:20:12,660
was gaining favor as a possible fuel.

341
00:20:12,661 --> 00:20:17,320
Identified in 1941, plutonium was almost twice as likely to

342
00:20:17,321 --> 00:20:21,760
undergo fission as uranium-235 and could be produced on a large

343
00:20:21,761 --> 00:20:24,560
scale by irradiating uranium in nuclear reactors.

344
00:20:26,520 --> 00:20:30,480
In order to produce plutonium, three production reactors were

345
00:20:30,481 --> 00:20:33,840
designed by engineers at the University of Chicago that would

346
00:20:33,841 --> 00:20:35,300
be built in Hanford, Washington.

347
00:20:37,580 --> 00:20:41,680
Groves initiated the construction at Hanford on August 27, 1943.

348
00:20:42,660 --> 00:20:45,600
force that had been recruited to the area.

349
00:20:45,700 --> 00:20:50,300
The number is as high as 60,000 construction workers living in

350
00:20:50,400 --> 00:20:52,600
tents out on the Hanford Reservation.

351
00:20:52,700 --> 00:20:56,500
So the government went in and bought up a half a million

352
00:20:56,600 --> 00:21:00,320
acres out in the desert of eastern Washington, created

353
00:21:00,420 --> 00:21:04,320
this remote construction site, and literally in a matter of

354
00:21:04,420 --> 00:21:09,260
months, constructed large-scale engineered structures for

355
00:21:09,261 --> 00:21:13,500
structures for plutonium production.

356
00:21:13,501 --> 00:21:16,800
But Groves was not content to rely on just two approaches,

357
00:21:16,801 --> 00:21:19,900
Y-12 and Hanford, to produce the weapons-grade fuel for the

358
00:21:19,901 --> 00:21:24,300
bomb. By September 1943, he had begun

359
00:21:24,301 --> 00:21:29,520
construction on a third, K-25, a gigantic gaseous diffusion

360
00:21:29,521 --> 00:21:34,420
plant at Oak Ridge. In the diffusion process, a

361
00:21:34,421 --> 00:21:37,820
gaseous compound, uranium hexafluoride, passes through a

362
00:21:37,821 --> 00:21:41,320
barriers, each one giving a slight enrichment of the lighter

363
00:21:41,321 --> 00:21:46,420
isotope U-235. The difficulty lay in finding

364
00:21:46,421 --> 00:21:49,920
a barrier that would not be degraded by the very corrosive

365
00:21:49,921 --> 00:21:53,920
gas. Scientists and engineers were

366
00:21:53,921 --> 00:21:57,760
not able to manufacture a satisfactory barrier until a year

367
00:21:57,761 --> 00:22:02,920
after site construction began on K-25, a building that would

368
00:22:02,921 --> 00:22:07,820
ultimately cover more area than any structure ever built.

369
00:22:07,821 --> 00:22:12,360
The U-shape measured half a mile long by 1,000 feet wide.

370
00:22:12,460 --> 00:22:15,460
With an area of 2 million square feet,

371
00:22:15,560 --> 00:22:19,160
it contained a series of sealed containers and cascades that

372
00:22:19,260 --> 00:22:23,160
ran the length of the building.

373
00:22:23,260 --> 00:22:26,480
Oak Ridge continued to grow, and by the end of the war,

374
00:22:26,580 --> 00:22:29,880
it was the fifth largest town in Tennessee.

375
00:22:29,980 --> 00:22:34,380
The total power consumption during the K-25 operation

376
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:36,920
during the war under Manhattan District

377
00:22:36,921 --> 00:22:40,260
was about 10% of the electrical power, electrical energy

378
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:42,760
in the United States.

379
00:22:42,860 --> 00:22:48,660
If you look at the size, the scope of the K-25 site

380
00:22:48,760 --> 00:22:51,160
in the Manhattan Project, I don't

381
00:22:51,260 --> 00:22:54,680
think there's been any engineering feat to date

382
00:22:54,780 --> 00:23:00,280
comparable to what was done within the time frame.

383
00:23:00,380 --> 00:23:01,980
It was looking less and less likely

384
00:23:02,080 --> 00:23:06,920
that enough U-235 could be produced to impact the war.

385
00:23:06,921 --> 00:23:10,160
At the same time, the alternative, plutonium,

386
00:23:10,260 --> 00:23:12,460
was proving to be equally tricky.

387
00:23:12,560 --> 00:23:14,720
Impurities in this new element were leading

388
00:23:14,820 --> 00:23:17,560
to increased neutron activity that would cause bombs

389
00:23:17,660 --> 00:23:21,000
to predetonate, to fizzle before the two halves joined

390
00:23:21,100 --> 00:23:22,440
in a critical mass.

391
00:23:22,540 --> 00:23:24,300
There are production problems at Oak Ridge.

392
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:26,740
They're not sure they can even make any uranium at that point,

393
00:23:26,840 --> 00:23:28,240
uranium-235.

394
00:23:28,340 --> 00:23:30,880
And so if they can't use plutonium in a gun,

395
00:23:30,980 --> 00:23:34,020
there may, in fact, not even be an atomic bomb.

396
00:23:34,120 --> 00:23:35,620
It's a real crisis.

397
00:23:35,621 --> 00:23:39,060
It's at that point that I think Oppenheimer's talent comes to

398
00:23:39,160 --> 00:23:42,020
the fore where he brings in the people, new people, in fact,

399
00:23:42,120 --> 00:23:45,360
and he reorganizes Los Alamos, but brings people in to solve

400
00:23:45,460 --> 00:23:48,360
the problem of how to make a plutonium bomb.

401
00:23:48,460 --> 00:23:51,360
The plutonium bomb's new configuration called for an

402
00:23:51,460 --> 00:23:54,200
outer shell of explosives that would direct symmetrical

403
00:23:54,300 --> 00:23:58,140
shock waves inward, compressing a subcritical central mass of

404
00:23:58,240 --> 00:23:59,280
plutonium.

405
00:23:59,380 --> 00:24:02,020
The resulting increase in the density would shrink distances

406
00:24:02,021 --> 00:24:05,320
distances between nuclei, thus starting the explosive chain

407
00:24:05,321 --> 00:24:08,060
reaction. Nobody had ever taken high

408
00:24:08,061 --> 00:24:11,260
explosives, wrapped them around something, and got a symmetrical

409
00:24:11,261 --> 00:24:13,160
explosion. And so they weren't even sure they

410
00:24:13,161 --> 00:24:15,660
could do that technically. It was a very, very tough

411
00:24:15,661 --> 00:24:19,060
engineering problem. So from May 1944 until about

412
00:24:19,061 --> 00:24:23,080
August of 1944, the laboratory wrestles with this problem.

413
00:24:23,081 --> 00:24:27,580
By late 1944, K25, the gaseous diffusion plant, was

414
00:24:27,581 --> 00:24:31,280
producing enriched U-235 that ran through the magnetic

415
00:24:31,281 --> 00:24:35,280
through the magnetic calutrons to effect a further enrichment.

416
00:24:35,281 --> 00:24:38,920
This one-two punch generated enough fuel for one gun-type

417
00:24:38,921 --> 00:24:41,920
bomb. But if, as military

418
00:24:41,921 --> 00:24:45,320
strategists thought, it would take more than one bomb to break

419
00:24:45,321 --> 00:24:50,000
the enemy's will, then it was crucial that the upcoming test

420
00:24:50,001 --> 00:24:55,200
of the new plutonium implosion bomb work.

421
00:24:55,201 --> 00:24:58,400
All this uncertainty only served to heighten tensions at Los

422
00:24:58,401 --> 00:25:01,140
as scientists and engineers prepared for the first

423
00:25:01,240 --> 00:25:03,740
detonation of an atomic weapon.

424
00:25:03,840 --> 00:25:06,040
If the implosion design was successful,

425
00:25:06,140 --> 00:25:07,840
it might bring an end to the war.

426
00:25:11,340 --> 00:25:13,740
As a substitute for copper, which was in short supply

427
00:25:13,840 --> 00:25:18,360
during World War II, the US Army borrowed almost 15,000 tons

428
00:25:18,460 --> 00:25:21,860
of silver from the US Treasury to wind into coils for the

429
00:25:21,960 --> 00:25:23,660
calutron's electromagnets.

430
00:25:23,661 --> 00:25:29,160
magnets. Weapon design for the uranium gun

431
00:25:29,161 --> 00:25:32,960
bomb was completed by February 1945.

432
00:25:32,961 --> 00:25:35,700
Confidence in the weapon was high.

433
00:25:35,701 --> 00:25:40,640
They named it Little Boy for its relatively small size, 10

434
00:25:40,641 --> 00:25:44,980
feet long and less than 10,000 pounds.

435
00:25:44,981 --> 00:25:48,780
Designers considered a test prior to combat use unnecessary

436
00:25:48,781 --> 00:25:53,660
and impossible, since there was only enough U-235 for one bomb.

437
00:25:53,661 --> 00:25:57,060
The design for the more complicated plutonium-fueled

438
00:25:57,061 --> 00:26:00,860
implosion device, dubbed Fat Man for its rotund shape, was

439
00:26:00,861 --> 00:26:07,300
approved in March, and a test was scheduled for July.

440
00:26:07,301 --> 00:26:10,420
In preparation for deployment of the weapons, Colonel Paul

441
00:26:10,421 --> 00:26:14,820
Tibbets, a veteran combat pilot with extensive B-29 experience,

442
00:26:14,821 --> 00:26:19,260
was selected and placed at the head of a new unit, the 509th

443
00:26:19,261 --> 00:26:22,720
Composite Group. The unit began training at Wendover

444
00:26:22,721 --> 00:26:30,720
field, Utah, dropping 5,500-pound orange dummy bombs.

445
00:26:30,721 --> 00:26:35,720
On April 12, 1945, President Roosevelt died in Warm Springs,

446
00:26:35,721 --> 00:26:39,720
Georgia, bringing Vice President Harry S. Truman into the Oval

447
00:26:39,721 --> 00:26:42,720
Office. Secrecy on the Manhattan Project

448
00:26:42,721 --> 00:26:45,720
had been so tight that Truman was not privy to the bomb

449
00:26:45,721 --> 00:26:48,720
developments and had to be briefed extensively in his first

450
00:26:48,721 --> 00:26:53,720
in office. Less than one month later,

451
00:26:53,721 --> 00:26:57,720
German armed forces in Europe surrendered.

452
00:26:57,721 --> 00:27:03,720
But Japan did not. In June 1945, Tibbets and his

453
00:27:03,721 --> 00:27:08,720
unit moved to Tinian Island in the Pacific, 1,450 miles from

454
00:27:08,721 --> 00:27:12,720
Tokyo, where the Navy Seabees had built the world's largest

455
00:27:12,721 --> 00:27:17,720
airport to accommodate Boeing's new B-29 superfortresses.

456
00:27:17,721 --> 00:27:22,480
The island was selected because it was within striking range of Japan.

457
00:27:23,300 --> 00:27:25,820
Number two, it had the longest runways.

458
00:27:26,220 --> 00:27:32,720
Number three, it had the facilities there to modify the area where we're going to load the bomb.

459
00:27:34,840 --> 00:27:41,620
I wanted to have my crews fly over enemy territory because they were not used to flying over enemy territory.

460
00:27:41,621 --> 00:27:47,020
I wanted to get practice flying, being shot at, flying alone.

461
00:27:47,720 --> 00:27:52,960
Half a world away on the morning of July 16, 1945, in the New

462
00:27:52,961 --> 00:27:57,480
Mexico desert 200 miles south of Los Alamos, scientists and

463
00:27:57,481 --> 00:28:01,020
dignitaries awaited the results of the first test of an atomic

464
00:28:01,021 --> 00:28:05,020
weapon. Oppenheimer had given the test

465
00:28:05,021 --> 00:28:09,620
of the implosion device the code name Trinity, a reference to a

466
00:28:09,621 --> 00:28:13,960
devotional poem by 17th century English poet John Donne.

467
00:28:13,961 --> 00:28:17,260
The poem explores the paradox of a god that destroys

468
00:28:17,360 --> 00:28:18,700
in order to renew.

469
00:28:18,800 --> 00:28:20,660
But on the morning of the Trinity test,

470
00:28:20,760 --> 00:28:23,700
the implosion device did not look particularly imposing

471
00:28:23,800 --> 00:28:26,600
atop a tower 100 feet high.

472
00:28:26,700 --> 00:28:29,480
One of the guys said that really the Trinity device

473
00:28:29,580 --> 00:28:32,620
owes a tremendous debt to 3M because most of it

474
00:28:32,720 --> 00:28:34,840
was held together with masking tape.

475
00:28:34,940 --> 00:28:38,080
It was a garage bomb, basically.

476
00:28:38,180 --> 00:28:40,260
And it literally takes about three days

477
00:28:40,360 --> 00:28:42,780
to assemble one of these wartime devices.

478
00:28:42,781 --> 00:28:45,020
devices. Think of them as crude laboratory

479
00:28:45,021 --> 00:28:46,960
devices. They're not production designs.

480
00:28:46,961 --> 00:28:49,180
You don't roll them off an assembly line.

481
00:28:49,181 --> 00:28:51,520
You've got to worry about, have we done everything right?

482
00:28:51,521 --> 00:28:55,720
What's missing? And there are stories where people,

483
00:28:55,721 --> 00:28:57,920
last minute, have a nightmare that they put something

484
00:28:57,921 --> 00:29:01,200
together wrong, actually go back in and discover that, oh,

485
00:29:01,201 --> 00:29:05,800
I inserted this wire backwards or that one.

486
00:29:05,801 --> 00:29:08,840
The scientists waited anxiously at their posts.

487
00:29:08,841 --> 00:29:12,780
Some feared success because of speculation that the bomb might

488
00:29:12,781 --> 00:29:15,680
the atmosphere and destroy the world.

489
00:29:15,780 --> 00:29:20,180
Teller was about 20 miles away at Campania Hill with Lawrence.

490
00:29:20,280 --> 00:29:23,520
And Teller mostly scared everybody to death

491
00:29:23,620 --> 00:29:28,760
because he was putting suntan lotion on his face and in his

492
00:29:28,860 --> 00:29:33,000
hands as a guard against the ultraviolet from the bomb.

493
00:29:33,100 --> 00:29:34,540
This is 20 miles away.

494
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:37,040
All the scientists were 20 miles away,

495
00:29:37,140 --> 00:29:39,380
but most feared a dud, especially

496
00:29:39,480 --> 00:29:41,740
since a blank test of the explosives surrounding

497
00:29:41,741 --> 00:29:43,740
had failed just days before.

498
00:29:43,741 --> 00:29:46,740
Sensors showed that they had not fired simultaneously

499
00:29:46,741 --> 00:29:49,740
and would not have compressed the core properly.

500
00:29:49,741 --> 00:29:52,740
It was really unnerving when the blank shot failed.

501
00:29:52,741 --> 00:29:57,740
The normal anxiety that one might have had with a device

502
00:29:57,741 --> 00:30:01,740
on which you had worked but had never been tested fully

503
00:30:01,741 --> 00:30:06,740
was heightened by the failure of the blank shot.

504
00:30:06,741 --> 00:30:08,740
General Groves was lying on the ground

505
00:30:08,741 --> 00:30:12,740
in the prone position, facing away from the blast,

506
00:30:12,840 --> 00:30:15,140
what he said was going through his mind was,

507
00:30:15,240 --> 00:30:18,820
what was he going to do when the timer got to zero

508
00:30:18,920 --> 00:30:20,020
and nothing happened?

509
00:30:29,620 --> 00:30:34,940
At 5.30 AM, July 16, 1945, the world

510
00:30:35,040 --> 00:30:36,900
entered the atomic age.

511
00:30:36,901 --> 00:30:41,600
with an intense flash, a sudden wave of heat, followed by a

512
00:30:41,601 --> 00:30:46,440
tremendous shock wave. The ball of fire extended 40,000

513
00:30:46,441 --> 00:30:50,440
feet. The bomb packed a punch equivalent

514
00:30:50,441 --> 00:30:55,860
to 20,000 tons of TNT, the high end of most of the scientists'

515
00:30:55,861 --> 00:31:01,060
predictions, completely vaporizing the steel tower

516
00:31:01,061 --> 00:31:06,900
and heating the desert sand into glass for a radius of 800 yards.

517
00:31:06,901 --> 00:31:11,640
What got me was I hadn't thought the heat of the fireball

518
00:31:11,641 --> 00:31:16,140
would heat my face exactly as at sunrise.

519
00:31:16,141 --> 00:31:19,240
It became brighter and brighter and rose.

520
00:31:19,241 --> 00:31:24,120
And I knew that soon it will be used over Japan,

521
00:31:24,121 --> 00:31:28,060
and then it will not be just an experiment.

522
00:31:28,061 --> 00:31:30,720
Groves returned to Washington to report the results

523
00:31:30,721 --> 00:31:33,400
to Secretary of War Henry Stimson

524
00:31:33,401 --> 00:31:36,640
preparations for the use of the bomb against Japan.

525
00:31:36,641 --> 00:31:39,480
Later that day, Groves had his photograph taken for the

526
00:31:39,481 --> 00:31:43,180
publicity that would accompany the bombing of Hiroshima.

527
00:31:43,181 --> 00:31:48,340
I said, the general looked down right there at Tokyo.

528
00:31:48,341 --> 00:31:51,280
He put his hands on his hips and says, no, I won't look at

529
00:31:51,281 --> 00:31:54,420
Tokyo, Ed, but I'll look somewhere close to it.

530
00:31:54,421 --> 00:31:58,660
So he did, and I made the picture.

531
00:31:58,661 --> 00:32:00,100
I don't know.

532
00:32:00,200 --> 00:32:05,500
Wherever he was looking was probably Hiroshima.

533
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:07,740
Although Trinity had been a success,

534
00:32:07,840 --> 00:32:10,940
questions remained about Fat Man.

535
00:32:11,040 --> 00:32:13,980
The gadget at Trinity was not a bomb.

536
00:32:14,080 --> 00:32:15,640
Couldn't have been dropped.

537
00:32:15,740 --> 00:32:17,840
It was a gadget sitting on top of a tower.

538
00:32:17,940 --> 00:32:20,580
So could the bomb work?

539
00:32:20,680 --> 00:32:22,280
That would certainly have been the military.

540
00:32:22,380 --> 00:32:26,420
We'd test them in action.

541
00:32:26,421 --> 00:32:29,620
Moreover, the untested Little Boy would only get one chance

542
00:32:29,720 --> 00:32:31,420
to work.

543
00:32:31,520 --> 00:32:33,020
The only way we could know for sure

544
00:32:33,120 --> 00:32:36,360
is if we actually test it, but we don't have enough uranium.

545
00:32:36,460 --> 00:32:38,560
So we're going to have to look at this and say,

546
00:32:38,660 --> 00:32:43,040
our calculations look good, our engineering looks good,

547
00:32:43,140 --> 00:32:46,140
but we don't know. And so Little Boy goes into combat

548
00:32:46,240 --> 00:32:50,920
as an untested weapon. The course of the war,

549
00:32:51,020 --> 00:32:54,120
the work of thousands, the expenditure of billions,

550
00:32:54,121 --> 00:32:58,820
and the fate of more than 100,000 Japanese soldiers and civilians

551
00:32:58,821 --> 00:33:02,700
hung in the balance on the morning of August 6, 1945,

552
00:33:02,701 --> 00:33:08,360
as the Enola Gay flew 31,000 feet over Hiroshima.

553
00:33:08,361 --> 00:33:12,900
On its way home from delivering the uranium bomb to Tinian

554
00:33:12,901 --> 00:33:17,300
Island, the USS Indianapolis was torpedoed and sunk by a Japanese

555
00:33:17,301 --> 00:33:21,020
submarine. Of the 1,196 men aboard,

556
00:33:21,021 --> 00:33:23,920
only 315 were saved.

557
00:33:24,120 --> 00:33:31,120
In June of 1945, 155 Manhattan Project scientists

558
00:33:31,220 --> 00:33:35,160
signed a petition calling for a demonstration of the atomic bomb

559
00:33:35,260 --> 00:33:39,640
over an uninhabited area prior to its use against Japan.

560
00:33:43,700 --> 00:33:45,640
General Groves heard about the petition

561
00:33:45,740 --> 00:33:49,080
and ordered that it not be circulated at Los Alamos.

562
00:33:49,180 --> 00:33:52,240
Then, to undermine the petition,

563
00:33:52,241 --> 00:33:56,840
he conducted a poll of atomic scientists.

564
00:33:56,940 --> 00:33:59,440
What do you think of a demonstration?

565
00:33:59,540 --> 00:34:04,320
To his chagrin, 83% of the people answering the poll

566
00:34:04,420 --> 00:34:07,920
said some sort of demonstration was preferable

567
00:34:08,020 --> 00:34:10,320
to bombing civilians.

568
00:34:10,420 --> 00:34:12,820
So then Grove saw to it that the petition

569
00:34:12,920 --> 00:34:18,700
was bottled up at Oak Ridge until after the bombs fell on Japan.

570
00:34:18,800 --> 00:34:21,200
There was, however, strong support,

571
00:34:21,201 --> 00:34:23,500
particularly at the highest levels of government

572
00:34:23,600 --> 00:34:26,000
and the military, to use the bomb swiftly

573
00:34:26,100 --> 00:34:28,500
against Japanese cities.

574
00:34:28,600 --> 00:34:31,740
There was every incentive to use this weapon

575
00:34:31,840 --> 00:34:34,420
to scare the Japanese into surrender

576
00:34:34,520 --> 00:34:38,320
before the Russians got in on the post-war settlement.

577
00:34:38,420 --> 00:34:41,160
What our military leaders feared

578
00:34:41,260 --> 00:34:44,320
was what happened in Korea, where there was a North Japan

579
00:34:44,420 --> 00:34:47,960
and a South Japan, and the North Japan was communist

580
00:34:48,060 --> 00:34:51,200
and the South Japan was capitalist.

581
00:34:51,201 --> 00:34:55,200
Moreover, the assault on the Japanese mainland,

582
00:34:55,201 --> 00:34:58,200
scheduled for November of 1945,

583
00:34:58,201 --> 00:35:01,200
would include a million and a half Allied soldiers.

584
00:35:01,201 --> 00:35:06,200
Casualties in the Pacific indicated that losses would be heavy.

585
00:35:06,201 --> 00:35:09,200
By the end of the Second World War,

586
00:35:09,201 --> 00:35:12,200
we were so angry at the Japanese.

587
00:35:12,201 --> 00:35:15,200
They had started the war.

588
00:35:15,201 --> 00:35:19,200
They had fought furiously and brutally.

589
00:35:19,201 --> 00:35:23,100
They had slaughtered civilians.

590
00:35:23,101 --> 00:35:28,000
We had, by that time, totally destroyed their Air Force.

591
00:35:28,001 --> 00:35:31,340
We had totally destroyed their Navy.

592
00:35:31,341 --> 00:35:33,340
And yet they wouldn't surrender.

593
00:35:37,560 --> 00:35:41,260
In the spring of 1945, General Groves chose

594
00:35:41,261 --> 00:35:44,000
populated Japanese cities as potential targets

595
00:35:44,001 --> 00:35:46,700
to demonstrate the bomb's destructive power

596
00:35:46,701 --> 00:35:49,600
and end the war as quickly as possible.

597
00:35:49,700 --> 00:35:53,000
Once selected, the cities on his shortlist were off limits

598
00:35:53,100 --> 00:35:55,000
to Allied bombing raids.

599
00:35:55,100 --> 00:35:59,640
They would be saved in order to be destroyed.

600
00:35:59,740 --> 00:36:02,240
The final decision rested with the president

601
00:36:02,340 --> 00:36:05,680
and was made on June 1st after a conversation with his Secretary

602
00:36:05,780 --> 00:36:08,180
of State, James Burns.

603
00:36:08,280 --> 00:36:11,220
And at one point, Burns said to Truman, Mr. President,

604
00:36:11,320 --> 00:36:14,220
what will you tell the American people at your impeachment

605
00:36:14,221 --> 00:36:18,560
in 1946, when they find out that you had a weapon

606
00:36:18,561 --> 00:36:22,720
that could have ended the war and saved American lives,

607
00:36:22,721 --> 00:36:25,560
and you decided not to use it.

608
00:36:25,561 --> 00:36:29,060
I think that probably was the decisive argument for Truman.

609
00:36:29,061 --> 00:36:33,780
He'd been in World War I, and he knew what it was to fight and die.

610
00:36:33,781 --> 00:36:36,980
And I think he was interested in stopping it as quick as he could,

611
00:36:36,981 --> 00:36:40,620
and I certainly admired the man for what he did.

612
00:36:40,621 --> 00:36:42,960
Let there be no mistake.

613
00:36:43,060 --> 00:36:47,360
We shall completely destroy Japan's power to make war.

614
00:36:47,460 --> 00:36:50,260
If they do not now accept our terms,

615
00:36:50,360 --> 00:36:54,260
they may expect a rain of ruin from the air, the like of which

616
00:36:54,360 --> 00:36:58,180
has never been seen on this Earth.

617
00:36:58,280 --> 00:37:02,180
At 2.45 AM on August 6th, Paul Tibbets

618
00:37:02,280 --> 00:37:06,380
lifted the Enola Gay off the runway on Tinian Island.

619
00:37:06,480 --> 00:37:08,380
I thought the airplane will probably

620
00:37:08,381 --> 00:37:10,620
will probably go down in history.

621
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:13,380
And I wanted to be sure that, in my mind,

622
00:37:13,480 --> 00:37:16,620
there could be no other B-29 that would have the same name.

623
00:37:16,720 --> 00:37:18,380
Well, my mother's name, Enola Gay,

624
00:37:18,480 --> 00:37:21,900
fit the bill because I knew there could not be two Enola

625
00:37:22,000 --> 00:37:24,300
Gays.

626
00:37:24,400 --> 00:37:30,140
On August 6, 1945, at 8.15 AM, the gun model uranium bomb

627
00:37:30,240 --> 00:37:34,800
Little Boy dropped from bomb bay doors of the Enola Gay.

628
00:37:34,801 --> 00:37:38,880
43 seconds later, the bomb detonated 1,900 feet above

629
00:37:38,881 --> 00:37:43,680
Hiroshima with a force of 12,500 tons of TNT.

630
00:37:43,681 --> 00:37:46,320
I could see little bits of Hiroshima down there,

631
00:37:46,321 --> 00:37:48,280
but I couldn't.

632
00:37:48,281 --> 00:37:52,520
There was nothing there but black boiling mess.

633
00:37:52,521 --> 00:37:54,380
And steam was coming up.

634
00:37:54,381 --> 00:37:56,520
There were bubbles, and that's the way that thing looked.

635
00:37:56,521 --> 00:38:03,520
90% of the city was leveled by the 500-mile-an-hour winds

636
00:38:03,521 --> 00:38:08,560
of the blast, which charred victims' skin two miles away

637
00:38:08,561 --> 00:38:12,300
and incinerated those directly beneath the detonation.

638
00:38:12,301 --> 00:38:14,400
We visited the patients.

639
00:38:14,401 --> 00:38:17,740
We visited the railroad platform, which they had lined up all

640
00:38:17,741 --> 00:38:21,540
the people who were dying of radiation sickness, maybe 600

641
00:38:21,541 --> 00:38:23,380
or 800, nothing to be done.

642
00:38:23,381 --> 00:38:26,520
They didn't have anything to go away with them.

643
00:38:26,521 --> 00:38:30,760
The small boy, I think was a boy, was bloated,

644
00:38:30,860 --> 00:38:33,760
and he couldn't hardly recognize the face at all.

645
00:38:33,860 --> 00:38:44,680
He said, OK-chan, OK-chan, mizu, mami, mami, water, water.

646
00:38:44,780 --> 00:38:48,680
The pitiful, painful cry is something I'll never forget.

647
00:38:51,880 --> 00:38:55,720
The Japanese death toll rose to 130,000 people killed

648
00:38:55,721 --> 00:38:59,720
killed by the blast and ensuing radiation sickness.

649
00:38:59,820 --> 00:39:02,920
Three days later, a second, more powerful plutonium bomb,

650
00:39:03,020 --> 00:39:07,760
Fat Man, fell on Nagasaki with similar results.

651
00:39:07,860 --> 00:39:11,660
Japan surrendered on August 14, 1945.

652
00:39:11,760 --> 00:39:18,680
Let us pray that peace be now restored to the world,

653
00:39:18,780 --> 00:39:23,180
and that God will preserve it always.

654
00:39:23,181 --> 00:39:26,720
The most catastrophic war in human history was over.

655
00:39:27,900 --> 00:39:30,400
I figured out how many of us at Los Alamos

656
00:39:30,401 --> 00:39:32,460
really knew all that was going on.

657
00:39:32,680 --> 00:39:34,540
And dividing that into the number of casualties,

658
00:39:34,720 --> 00:39:38,620
I figure that I'm responsible for 100 or 200 of them.

659
00:39:39,220 --> 00:39:43,620
And that's a hard kind of thing to be responsible for.

660
00:39:44,780 --> 00:39:50,500
But I've been asked, in view of that calculation,

661
00:39:50,501 --> 00:39:51,540
would I do it again?

662
00:39:51,541 --> 00:39:55,980
And the answer is yes, because any new weapon,

663
00:39:57,100 --> 00:40:00,960
I would want to be given first to the United States.

664
00:40:01,900 --> 00:40:05,120
The American atomic weapon monopoly was short-lived.

665
00:40:05,900 --> 00:40:09,540
In 1949, the Soviets detonated an atomic bomb.

666
00:40:11,540 --> 00:40:16,380
During the 1950s, both superpowers added the hydrogen bomb to their arsenals

667
00:40:16,381 --> 00:40:19,120
in an attempt to achieve military superiority.

668
00:40:19,121 --> 00:40:22,920
Ultimately, the two would have tens of thousands of weapons

669
00:40:23,020 --> 00:40:24,260
in their nuclear arsenals.

670
00:40:24,360 --> 00:40:25,800
GERALD REINER, U.S.

671
00:40:25,900 --> 00:40:32,060
Progress cannot be stopped, and we are told to stop progress.

672
00:40:34,300 --> 00:40:39,180
I am very much afraid that progress will occur

673
00:40:39,280 --> 00:40:44,420
and will occur in a country that is less dedicated to peace

674
00:40:44,520 --> 00:40:46,340
than we are.

675
00:40:46,341 --> 00:40:49,780
But Oppenheimer did not agree that the arms race

676
00:40:49,781 --> 00:40:51,580
represented progress.

677
00:40:51,581 --> 00:40:54,780
His opposition to Edward Teller's hydrogen bomb project

678
00:40:54,781 --> 00:40:57,580
and questions about his communist sympathies

679
00:40:57,581 --> 00:40:59,980
led to the revoking of his security clearance

680
00:40:59,981 --> 00:41:02,860
at a government hearing in 1954.

681
00:41:02,861 --> 00:41:06,300
Teller testified against his former chief.

682
00:41:06,301 --> 00:41:09,060
He should not have testified against Oppenheimer.

683
00:41:09,061 --> 00:41:11,460
I think that the whole proceeding against Oppenheimer

684
00:41:11,461 --> 00:41:15,340
was a terrible mistake on the part of the United States

685
00:41:15,341 --> 00:41:20,340
States government, and I felt very upset by it,

686
00:41:20,440 --> 00:41:22,940
as I think many scientists did.

687
00:41:23,040 --> 00:41:27,420
And I felt that any scientist who supported this was more or

688
00:41:27,520 --> 00:41:33,000
less doing exactly the wrong thing.

689
00:41:33,100 --> 00:41:37,200
Oppenheimer's career had been brought to an end.

690
00:41:37,300 --> 00:41:39,660
But the debate over his and the Manhattan Project's

691
00:41:39,760 --> 00:41:43,660
contribution to history was just beginning.

692
00:41:43,661 --> 00:41:49,380
If you graph human deaths from war across the last 200 years,

693
00:41:49,381 --> 00:41:53,340
you will see an exponential increase up to 1945,

694
00:41:54,020 --> 00:41:58,200
at which point the line on the graph drops to about a million a year

695
00:41:58,201 --> 00:42:00,040
and stays there for the rest of the century.

696
00:42:01,020 --> 00:42:07,580
Clearly, there was a radical break in international affairs in 1945,

697
00:42:08,580 --> 00:42:13,240
and I think that break has to be attributed to this discovery that scientists made.

698
00:42:13,660 --> 00:42:16,140
working peacefully in their laboratories,

699
00:42:17,140 --> 00:42:21,440
trying to understand how the world really works

700
00:42:21,441 --> 00:42:25,140
rather than how we would wish that it worked.

701
00:42:27,340 --> 00:42:30,200
The question of the Manhattan Project's legacy,

702
00:42:31,020 --> 00:42:33,580
whether the atom bomb produced under the strain of war

703
00:42:33,581 --> 00:42:36,520
by some of the greatest scientific minds of the last century

704
00:42:36,521 --> 00:42:39,320
should be seen paradoxically as a peacemaker

705
00:42:39,321 --> 00:42:42,260
or simply as a savage destructive weapon,

706
00:42:42,261 --> 00:42:47,760
remains as polarizing today as it was 50 years ago.

707
00:42:47,840 --> 00:42:51,300
But for some, this debate is not abstract.

708
00:42:51,360 --> 00:42:55,800
The legacy of August 6, 1945 is far too personal.

709
00:42:55,880 --> 00:43:01,240
Every year on this day, on 815, I always offer prayer.

710
00:43:01,320 --> 00:43:04,680
And even this morning, tears flow.

711
00:43:04,740 --> 00:43:06,180
I just couldn't.

712
00:43:06,260 --> 00:43:08,420
Everything comes right back at me.

713
00:43:08,421 --> 00:43:12,120
And of course, I can't forget all the people that

714
00:43:12,220 --> 00:43:13,660
was killed in that bomb.

715
00:43:19,260 --> 00:43:20,860
But perhaps the most lasting effect

716
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:24,360
of the Manhattan Project?

717
00:43:24,460 --> 00:43:27,240
I can't recall an event that had such a decisive effect

718
00:43:27,340 --> 00:43:29,940
on American society or all societies,

719
00:43:30,040 --> 00:43:31,440
because for the first time, they

720
00:43:31,540 --> 00:43:34,280
faced instant, total, absolute annihilation,

721
00:43:34,380 --> 00:43:36,580
and one for which the average citizen

722
00:43:36,581 --> 00:43:39,580
and no material way to intervene to prevent it.

723
00:43:39,581 --> 00:43:42,580
It shattered this sense of security

724
00:43:42,581 --> 00:43:45,580
and of confidence in American society,

725
00:43:45,581 --> 00:43:48,580
I think, unlike any other event in our history.

